    "record_id": "span:opus5-2608.14798v1/sampling-alg",
    "record_type": "agtxiv.v3.source-span/0.0.0",
    "revision": 1
   },
   {
    "content_hash": "sha256:145ba53dec81c6bd19a41f2c953371f017f9df68076c9e98837208ce54d20362",
    "record_id": "span:opus5-2608.14798v1/estimator-bounds",
    "record_type": "agtxiv.v3.source-span/0.0.0",
    "revision": 1
   },
   {
    "content_hash": "sha256:2788ba66621fee77f01194dc7da56936f9fab0f510ec5f9b428999d7298a0016",
    "record_id": "span:opus5-2608.14798v1/estimator-conc",
    "record_type": "agtxiv.v3.source-span/0.0.0",
    "revision": 1
   },
   {
    "content_hash": "sha256:70a0f2d15b2b6a97f4d6941d6eefaa4af275e30088e7ed19441a3419c6cc53c0",
    "record_id": "span:opus5-2608.14798v1/abstract",
    "record_type": "agtxiv.v3.source-span/0.0.0",
    "revision": 1
   }
  ],
  "statement": "Theorem (SPF complexity, informal): for fixed $k$ and $\\beta$, the truncated SPF $\\mathcal{Z}^{(k)}_\\beta(\\psi)$ can be estimated to additive error $\\epsilon$ and failure probability $\\delta$ using the Bell-sampling primitive that estimates Pauli-spectrum moments; approximating the normalized full SPF further requires choosing $k$ so the truncation remainder is below the target accuracy. The supplement gives $\\mathcal{N}=\\mathcal{O}(\\cosh^2\\beta\\,e^{-2\\beta}\\epsilon^{-2}\\log(1/\\delta))$ samples for the normalized truncation, and $\\mathcal{N}=\\mathcal{O}(\\cosh^2\\beta\\,e^{-2\\beta}(\\epsilon-\\Delta(\\beta,k))^{-2}\\log(1/\\delta))$ for the full normalized SPF, valid only for $\\epsilon>\\Delta(\\beta,k)$.",
  "system": "$n$-qubit pure states, $\\mathcal{H}_n\\cong(\\mathbb{C}^2)^{\\otimes n}$, $d_n=2^n$, finite $n$"
 },
